"""Merges the configuration changes of an upstream release into the LFOps templates.

Every template listed in tools/follow-upstream.yml is a copy of a packaged configuration
file with LFOps changes on top, and names the package release it is based on in a Jinja
comment in its header (`{# Based on graylog-server 7.2.1 #}`), which does not end up in
the deployed file. For a new release, this tool extracts /etc from the RPM and the DEB
of the release (in a podman container, after checking the package signature against the
application's `gpg_key`), plus the templated files the package ships outside of /etc,
and:

1. Checks the packaging, on every run: files below /etc that are neither templated
   nor ignored in tools/follow-upstream.yml, templated files the release no longer ships,
   files whose content differs between RPM and DEB, and install scripts that differ from
   those of the base release in more than the version (they may modify the file on the
   host).
2. Merges the upstream changes into every template not based on this release yet:

       git merge-file <template> <RPM file, base release> <RPM file, new release>

   The upstream changes between the two releases land in the template, the LFOps
   changes are kept. Where both touch the same lines, the template gets conflict
   markers. A Debian family template whose packaged file differs from the RPM one is
   merged from the DEB file instead (MongoDB's mongod.conf).

   * Same release series (`7.2.0` -> `7.2.1`): the template is merged in place.
   * New release series (`7.2.1` -> `7.3.0`): the template of the latest older series
     is merged into a new template, and the series is added to the supported versions.
   * No change between the packaged files: only the `Based on` line is updated, so the
     template records that the release was checked.

With `--check`, the tool lists the releases in the RPM repositories that are newer than
the templates' `Based on` lines, without fetching or writing anything. Applications whose
repository URL needs an environment variable that is not set (`${TOKEN}`) are skipped.

Pre-release builds (`7.3.0-1.alpha.1`) are ignored by `--check` and refused by a run,
unless `--allow-prerelease` is given. Some vendors keep the marker in the package
release only, so the package version alone (`7.3.0`) looks like a final release.

Exit code 0: nothing to review, or no new release. 1: conflicts or packaging findings to
review, or new releases found. 2: the run could not complete. Run this tool from the
repository root directory. It never commits.
"""

import argparse
import datetime
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess  # nosec B404 - runs podman and git, see the calls below
import sys

import yaml

__author__ = 'Linuxfabrik GmbH, Zurich/Switzerland'
